    <paragraph>For the following exercises, consider the polar graph below. Give two sets of polar coordinates for each point.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="132" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Coordinates of point A.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="133" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Coordinates of point B.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$B ( 3 , - \pi 3 ) B ( -3 , 2 \pi 3 ) B ( 3 , - \pi 3 ) B ( -3 , 2 \pi 3 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="134" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Coordinates of point C.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="135" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Coordinates of point D.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$D ( 5 , 7 \pi 6 ) D ( -5 , \pi 6 ) D ( 5 , 7 \pi 6 ) D ( -5 , \pi 6 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, the rectangular coordinates of a point are given. Find two sets of polar coordinates for the point in $$\left( {0,2\pi} \right\rbrack.$$ Round to three decimal places.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="136" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {2, 2} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="137" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {3,-4} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 5 , 5 . 356 ) ( - 5 , 2 . 214 ) ( 5 , 5 . 356 ) ( - 5 , 2 . 214 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="138" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {8, 15} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="139" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {-6, 8} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 10 , 2 . 214 ) ( - 10 , 5 . 356 ) ( 10 , 2 . 214 ) ( - 10 , 5 . 356 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="140" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {4, 3} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="141" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {3,-\sqrt{3}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 2 3 , 11 \pi 6 ) ( -2 3 , 5 \pi 6 ) ( 2 3 , 11 \pi 6 ) ( -2 3 , 5 \pi 6 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, find rectangular coordinates for the given point in polar coordinates.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="142" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {2,\frac{5\pi}{4}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="143" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {-2,\frac{\pi}{6}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( - 3 , -1 ) ( - 3 , -1 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="144" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {5,\frac{\pi}{3}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="145" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {1,\frac{7\pi}{6}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( - 3 2 , -1 2 ) ( - 3 2 , -1 2 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="146" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {-3,\frac{3\pi}{4}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="147" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {0,\frac{\pi}{2}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 0 , 0 ) ( 0 , 0 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="148" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {-4.5,6.5} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, determine whether the graphs of the polar equation are symmetric with respect to the $$x$$ -axis, the $$y$$ -axis, or the origin.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="149" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 3 \sin (2\theta)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Symmetry with respect to the x-axis, y-axis, and origin.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="150" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r^{2} = 9 \cos \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="151" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = \cos \left( \frac{\theta}{5} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Symmetric with respect to x-axis only.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="152" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 2 \sec \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="153" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 1 + \cos \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Symmetry with respect to x-axis only.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, describe the graph of each polar equation. Confirm each description by converting into a rectangular equation.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="154" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="155" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Line $$y=xy=x$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="156" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = \sec \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="157" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = \csc \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$y = 1 y = 1$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, convert the rectangular equation to polar form and sketch its graph.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="158" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="159" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x^{2} - y^{2} = 16$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Hyperbola; polar form $$r2cos(2\theta )=16r2cos(2\theta )=16$$ or $$r2=16sec(2\theta ).r2=16sec(2\theta ).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="160" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = 8$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, convert the rectangular equation to polar form and sketch its graph.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="161" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$3x - y = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$r=23cos\theta -sin\theta r=23cos\theta -sin\theta$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="162" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y^{2} = 4x$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, convert the polar equation to rectangular form and sketch its graph.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="163" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 4 \sin \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$x2+y2=4yx2+y2=4y$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="164" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 6 \cos \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="165" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$xtanx2+y2=yxtanx2+y2=y$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="166" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = \cot \theta \csc \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, sketch a graph of the polar equation and identify any symmetry.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="167" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 1 + \sin \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>y-axis symmetry</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="168" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 3 - 2 \cos \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="169" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 2 - 2 \sin \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>y-axis symmetry</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="170" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 5 - 4 \sin \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="171" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 3 \cos \left( {2\theta} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>x- and y-axis symmetry and symmetry about the pole</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="172" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 3 \sin \left( {2\theta} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="173" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 2 \cos \left( {3\theta} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>x-axis symmetry</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="174" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 3 \cos \left( \frac{\theta}{2} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="175" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r^{2} = 4 \cos \left( {2\theta} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>x- and y-axis symmetry and symmetry about the pole</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="176" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r^{2} = 4 \sin \theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="177" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$r = 2\theta$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>no symmetry</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="178" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The graph of $$r = 2 \cos (2\theta)\sec (\theta).$$ is called a strophoid. Use a graphing utility to sketch the graph, and, from the graph, determine the asymptote.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="179" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use a graphing utility and sketch the graph of $$r = \frac{6}{2 \sin \theta - 3 \cos \theta}.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>a line</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="180" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use a graphing utility to graph $$r = \frac{1}{1 - \cos \theta}.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="181" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use technology to graph $$r = e^{\sin (\theta)} - 2 \cos \left( {4\theta} \right).$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="182" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use technology to plot $$r = \sin \left( \frac{3\theta}{7} \right)$$ (use the interval $$0 \leq \theta \leq 14\pi).$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="183" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Without using technology, sketch the polar curve $$\theta = \frac{2\pi}{3}.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="184" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use a graphing utility to plot $$r = \theta \sin \theta$$ for $$-\pi \leq \theta \leq \pi.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="185" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use technology to plot $$r = e^{-0.1\theta}$$ for $$-10 \leq \theta \leq 10.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="186" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] There is a curve known as the “Black Hole.” Use technology to plot $$r = e^{-0.01\theta}$$ for $$-100 \leq \theta \leq 100.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="187" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Use the results of the preceding two problems to explore the graphs of $$r = e^{-0.001\theta}$$ and $$r = e^{-0.0001\theta}$$ for $$|\theta| &gt; 100.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Answers vary. One possibility is the spiral lines become closer together and the total number of spirals increases.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
